{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_12-22-18-hashicorp_raft-real-run && python3 -c \\\"\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nfor i in s['interpretations']:\n    if i['id']=='575c827cea9b4f07a8a4f7fbcf83986d-interpretation-0':\n        print(i['rationale'])\n\\\" | head -5\"","aggregated_output":"The question asks whether the follower keeps an in-memory last log index its store no longer holds and whether ordinary AppendEntries can bring it back; both halves are answered, and the answer to the second is now sharper than refusal alone. After the conflicting append with the store failure armed (receipt 3448bd1c64244c3b884b24c7bad8a8bf, injected case, conflict index 1) the node's last log index was 3 while its store held only index 1 and no longer held index 2, and it answered Success false with that stale index. Ordinary AppendEntries then go two ways. The whole-log append from index 1 - the shape the leader's builder produces once its rejections have walked nextIndex back to one - was refused (index 2, Success false, store still at index 1), because the conflict loop trusts the stale in-memory index, looks the missing entry up in the store, misses and returns. But the shape a leader sends while its nextIndex is still one past that stale index, a single entry 4 behind previous index 3, was accepted with Success true (index 3): the store now holds index 4 above a gap at 2 and 3, the node's last log index is 4, and the leader reading that response would take the peer as caught up. The control accepted both shapes and kept a contiguous store from 1 to 4 (indices 5 to 7), so the injected failure is what produced the gap. The mechanism is the same source path in all three phases: the abort returns before setLastLog, the in-memory last log stays ahead of the store, the conflict loop treats any index above it as new without reading the store, and the abort also refreshes no contact. What remains source-read rather than measured is a live leader walking its nextIndex down and the effect of the stale index on the node's own vote claims, which the vote path takes from the same in-memory fields.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
